ent\"]}\n99: {\"step_index\":100,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:33:04Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Decomposing the Roman Domination Task**\\n\\nThe goal is to prove an inequality involving radius and the Roman domination number of a graph. The statement requires checking that if a graph is nontrivial and has a radius, the radius plus one is bounded above by the Roman domination number. The overall structure will be a universal quantification with an implication.\\n\\n\\n**Exploring the Inequality Proof**\\n\\nThe goal is to prove `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`, given a Roman dominating function. It's unclear if this holds universally, especially considering the case `W ≥ Fintype.card V`. The analysis considers if the radius is bounded above by `Fintype.card V - 1`, aiming to leverage the relationship between the radius and the graph's cardinality. Reviewing the definition of radius and eccentricity will be helpful.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing BallWithin Growth**\\n\\nConsidering if `ballWithin G (n - 1) v = univ` holds, given $|V| = n$. The conjecture relies on the longest simple path length being at most `n - 1`. The recursive definition of `ballWithin` appears to provide a way to build towards a proof of this.\\n\\nIf `ballWithin G k v ≠ univ`, and given a connected graph,  the cardinality of `ballWithin G (k+1) v` is strictly larger than `ballWithin G k v`. This is because there must be an edge between `ballWithin G k v` and its complement.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\\\\nimport glob\\\\nfiles = glob.glob(\\\\\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/**/*.lean\\\\\\\", recursive=True)\\\\nfor f in files:\\\\n    if \\\\\\\"Metric\\\\\\\" in f or \\\\\\\"Distance\\\\\\\" in f or \\\\\\\"Connected\\\\\\\" in f or